      "title": "9.8.4 Salts of weak acids and weak bases",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 205,
          "pdf_to": 205,
          "note": "Topic 9.8.4, printed page 205."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For salts derived from weak acids and weak bases (e.g., ammonium acetate, NH₄CH₃COO):"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The conjugate acid (NH₄⁺) and the conjugate base (CH₃COO⁻) both affect the pH."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{NH4CH3COO_{(aq)} <=> NH4^+_{(aq)} + CH3COO^-_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "NH₄CH₃COO(aq) ⇌ NH₄⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "NH₄⁺ hydrolyzes to produce H₃O⁺."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{NH4^+_{(aq)} + H2O_{(l)} <=> NH3_{(aq)} + H3O^+_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "NH₄⁺(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₃(aq) + H₃O⁺(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "CH₃COO⁻ hydrolyzes to produce OH⁻."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CH3COO^-_{(aq)} + H2O_{(aq)} <=> CH3COOH_{(aq)} + OH^-_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃COO⁻(aq) + H₂O(aq) ⇌ CH₃COOH(aq) + OH⁻(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The resultant pH of the solution depends on the relative strengths of the conjugate acid and conjugate base. The solution may be acidic, basic, or nearly neutral, depending on which reaction is more dominant."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
